- ## Step 6 - Corporate TLS interception - the silent blocker
- (`cas-bridge.xethub.hf.co`). A corporate TLS-inspecting proxy intercepts that
- host (and ModelScope) with its own certificate, so downloads fail with:
- # issuer=... O = <your corporate proxy vendor> Root CA  -> interception confirmed
- Fix: install the corporate root CA (already trusted by Windows) into the distro.
- $out = "$env:USERPROFILE\corp-ca.crt"
-   Where-Object { $_.Subject -like '*<your corporate proxy name>*' -or $_.Issuer -like '*<your corporate proxy name>*' } |
- (Replace `<your corporate proxy name>` with your actual proxy vendor. Do NOT
- commit this cert to any repo - it is corporate property.)
- tr -d '\r' < /mnt/c/Users/<you>/corp-ca.crt > /tmp/corp-ca.pem
- awk '/BEGIN CERTIFICATE/{n++} /BEGIN CERTIFICATE/,/END CERTIFICATE/{print > ("/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/corp-" n ".crt")}' /tmp/corp-ca.pem
- Then for every Python/HF command, export (Python uses certifi, which lacks the
- corporate root):
- ## Step 7 - Proxy env vars hijack localhost
- On a proxied machine, WSL inherits `HTTP_PROXY`/`http_proxy` from a corporate PAC.
- Requests to the local ACE-Step API (`127.0.0.1:8001`) then get routed to the proxy
- and come back as a 403 block page. Bypass the proxy for local calls:
- | `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` on `cas-bridge.xethub.hf.co` / modelscope | Corporate proxy TLS interception | Install corporate root CA (Step 6) + `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`/`SSL_CERT_FILE` + `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1` |
